{
  // TypeScript project #7 — the Vitest suites under scripts/ (#635).
  //
  // These were the one body of TypeScript in the repo that nothing type-checked.
  // The cost was not hypothetical: facets.test.ts's makeFacets stub passed a
  // `multiOnly` the service never declared and omitted the REQUIRED `postFolders`,
  // so the 'folder' facet row model had no coverage at all while the suite stayed
  // green. A stub that drifts from the contract tests a shape the app never passes,
  // and it does it without ever going red.
  //
  // Why a separate project rather than folding *.test.ts into scripts/tsconfig.json:
  // that project is a standalone-Node CommonJS runtime (nodenext, .cts, run un-built
  // by Node's type stripping). The suites are a different runtime — Vitest transpiles
  // them through Vite, and they import ACROSS layers (renderer services, main-process
  // modules, extension utils) whose sources are written for bundler resolution and
  // explicit .ts specifiers. Measured both ways: nodenext leaves 70 suites red purely
  // on module-format grounds, bundler leaves 53. So this project mirrors
  // app/tsconfig.web.json (the settings the imported renderer sources are written
  // for) plus Node types, since the suites themselves run under Node.
  //
  // The ambient renderer .d.ts files are pulled in explicitly: HologramPost /
  // HologramUserAgg / HologramFolder are globals declared there, and they are the
  // shapes the stubs are supposed to match.
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "lib": ["ES2022", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "jsx": "react-jsx",
    "noEmit": true,
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true,
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "strict": true,
    // noImplicitAny / useUnknownInCatchVariables stay off for the same reason as
    // every other project here (see app/tsconfig.node.json).
    "noImplicitAny": false,
    "useUnknownInCatchVariables": false,
    "noUncheckedSideEffectImports": false,
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    // Same both-candidates typeRoots as scripts/tsconfig.json — npm is free to hoist
    // @types to the repo root or nest it under app/, and a missing entry is ignored.
    // extension/node_modules/@types is here for "chrome" below: a suite that
    // imports an extension util (save-history.test.ts) pulls chrome.* in with it,
    // and without the ambient declarations that read as `Cannot find name 'chrome'`
    // — cause (b) in the exclusion list below, which is why several suites are
    // quarantined there. This does not un-quarantine them (they stay excluded for
    // their own reasons), it just stops the cause from applying to new ones.
    "typeRoots": ["../node_modules/@types", "../app/node_modules/@types", "../extension/node_modules/@types"],
    "types": ["node", "chrome"],
    // Mirrors app/tsconfig.web.json's alias for '@'. It does NOT mirror that
    // project's `electron` redirect: the renderer program needs the shim because
    // electron's own .d.ts carries a Node reference that would shadow the DOM
    // lib's timers, and a browser-only program must not have Node globals. This
    // project already declares "node" above (the suites run under Node), so the
    // shadowing that redirect prevents cannot happen here — while the redirect
    // itself DID break the other half: the suites that reach app/src/main get
    // ipcMain / BrowserWindow / dialog / utilityProcess resolved against a
    // renderer shim that declares none of them. Paths are relative to THIS file.
    "paths": {
      "@/*": ["../app/src/renderer/src/*"]
    }
  },
  // Glob, not a hand-written list: a NEW suite is type-checked the moment it lands.
  // That is the point of the whole project — the exclusions below are existing debt,
  // not a policy, and nothing new is allowed to join them silently.
  //
  // extension/types/globals.d.ts is pulled in for the same reason as the renderer's
  // ambient files: a suite that reaches an extension util gets that util's own Window
  // augmentation (__hologramAutoCapture) from it, and without the file the augmentation
  // reads as a property the DOM's Window does not have.
  "include": ["**/*.test.ts", "vitest.setup.ts", "vitest.global-setup.ts", "lib-capture-rig.ts", "lib-user-event.ts", "vite-client.d.ts", "../app/src/renderer/src/types/**/*.d.ts", "../extension/types/globals.d.ts"],
  // 35 of the 182 suites do not compile yet; the other 147 are checked. TWO causes
  // remain, and only the second is the drift this project exists to catch:
  //
  //   (a) imports a .cts module that exports via `module.exports = {...}` (5 suites) —
  //       scripts' own lib-sandbox-instance.cts, gen-extension-tokens.cts,
  //       lib-schema-canary.cts and friends. They read as having no named exports:
  //       TS2459 on a direct import, TS2339 through require() or a dynamic import. Not
  //       drift; a module-format gap, and the one cause that will keep catching NEW
  //       suites, because a .cts helper next to a .ts suite is still a normal shape in
  //       scripts/.
  //
  //       ⚠️ THE OBVIOUS FIX DOES NOT WORK, measured on 2026-08-08: a second project on
  //       `module: nodenext` reports the SAME TS2459. TypeScript does not infer
  //       CommonJS exports from a `module.exports = {...}` assignment in a .ts/.cts
  //       file at all — that inference is for JS files (allowJs), and no module setting
  //       turns it on. Nor can the sources declare it: `export =` is the construct tsc
  //       would understand, and `erasableSyntaxOnly` (set by scripts/tsconfig.json,
  //       because those files run un-built under Node's type stripping) rejects it.
  //       Not a tsconfig change: do not reopen this by editing this file.
  //
  //       THE FIX THAT WORKS is to make the directory ESM, and #1052 did it for
  //       native-host/ — the five .cts modules there became .mts with real
  //       `export`s, and **16 suites came off this list in one move** (51 → 35).
  //       Node's require(esm) is what made it legal: the Electron main process still
  //       loads install.mts as raw source by computed path, synchronously, from a CJS
  //       bundle. scripts/ is the same move waiting to happen (91 .cts files, 16 of
  //       them with module.exports), and it is what would retire cause (a) for good.
  //   (b) fixtures that have drifted from the ambient shapes (30 suites) — HologramPost /
  //       HologramUserAgg / HologramQueryLeaf / HologramNavEntry / ModelRegistryEntry
  //       literals missing required members, wrong arity, wrong element types. THIS is
  //       the real debt, and each one is an instance of exactly the bug #635 was filed
  //       about.
  //
  //       How query.test.ts came off the list (the pattern, 139 errors → 0): its fixture
  //       helpers already existed but had NO RETURN TYPE, so `kind` widened to `string`
  //       and every call site quietly stopped matching. Annotating three helpers killed
  //       85% of the errors at once. What the annotation then EXPOSED is the part that
  //       matters: the poster fixture carried 6 of HologramUserAgg's 14 fields, and
  //       filling in the rest turned a passing test RED — `instances[]` is what
  //       posterPredOf matches on, so a fixture that set only the singular `instance`
  //       had been asserting against a shape buildUsers never produces. Annotate the
  //       helper first, then read what goes red; that is the bug, not the annotation.
  //
  // THREE causes are gone (2026-08-08), and they took 33 suites off this list with
  // them. None of the three was a property of the suites:
  //   - the native-host half of cause (a), 16 suites: #1052 made that directory ESM.
  //     Nothing in those suites changed — they were quarantined for the shape of the
  //     modules they import, and the modules changed shape.
  //   - the electron one (was cause (c)): the `electron` entry in `paths` redirected
  //     app/src/main's ipcMain / BrowserWindow / dialog / utilityProcess to the
  //     renderer's shim, which declares none of them. Dropping the redirect (see the
  //     compilerOptions comment for why this project never needed it) took 15 errors
  //     out of app/src/main/** — none of which were in a suite at all.
  //   - the Chrome/Vite ambient one (was cause (b)): `types: ["chrome"]` had already
  //     covered chrome.*. What was left was two declarations, both now included above —
  //     the Window augmentation in extension/types/globals.d.ts, and the `?inline` CSS
  //     specifier that extension/utils/tokens.ts uses, which the suites reach through
  //     background.ts → inject-failure.ts and which vite-client.d.ts declares (that
  //     file's header says why it cannot be a `types` entry).
  //
  // Removing a name from this list is the unit of work. The counts above are MEASURED,
  // not maintained by hand: empty this `exclude`, run tsc -p on this file, and every
  // suite that reports no error of its own can come off the list.
  "exclude": [
    "archive-db-export.test.ts",
    "archive-db-import.test.ts",
    "background-wiring.test.ts",
    "bridge-ssrf.test.ts",
    "bulk-capture.test.ts",
    "capture-overlay.test.ts",
    "content-fixtures.test.ts",
    "contrast-parity.test.ts",
    "db-schema.test.ts",
    "db-write.test.ts",
    "db.test.ts",
    "electron-path-guard.test.ts",
    // Cause (a), on scripts/gen-extension-tokens.cts. The three errors that are not
    // TS2459 are that same gap's fallout: build()'s return type is lost with the
    // export, so its Maps read as Map<unknown, unknown>.
    "extension-tokens.test.ts",
    "folder-nesting.test.ts",
    "folders-merge.test.ts",
    "image-zoom.test.ts",
    "lib-db-integrity.test.ts",
    "listing.test.ts",
    "mastodon-url.test.ts",
    "metadata-correctness.test.ts",
    "metadata-origin.test.ts",
    "migrate.test.ts",
    // #832: the registry fixture omits ModelRegistryEntry's required `purpose` — cause
    // (b). (It was quarantined for the electron pull through lib-ml-runtime.ts →
    // native-host.ts; that cause is gone, this one is its own.)
    "model-manager.test.ts",
    // Cause (b), and #1052 sharpened it rather than fixing it: PostRecordInput's
    // `media` is now Partial<MediaItemShape>[] instead of a shallow Partial's full
    // MediaItemShape[], so what is left here is the fixture passing a number where a
    // string goes and a null where an item goes.
    "post-record.test.ts",
    "records.test.ts",
    "sandbox-instance.test.ts",
    "sandbox-real-seed.test.ts",
    "save-error-i18n.test.ts",
    "save-folder-guard.test.ts",
    "save-post-video.test.ts",
    "schema-canary.test.ts",
    "tabstate.test.ts",
    "transfer-roundtrip.test.ts",
    "users.test.ts",
    "verify-record.test.ts"
  ]
}
